#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "177a0110-424b-5ac3-a24e-e817cbcf479c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21823" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} \\cos(9x)\\cos(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{20}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/id #uuid "ebf57ac7-29e3-595e-8a72-032e10c470aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21824" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(12x)\\sin(5x)\\,\\text{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{34}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(17x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "14466847-40e1-5a4f-b50a-23b62590f0a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21825" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\sin(2x)\\cos(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-1)+\\frac{1}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a371de4-7c3f-5687-bd3a-d3ab5ab803ce" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c202af47-5cc0-5423-9bc1-f59200a5ab79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\sin (4x) - \\frac{1}{12}\\sin (6x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164855} {:answer/id #uuid "e15fb7b1-c405-521a-9c0c-d940a9b38c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (x) - \\frac{1}{5}\\sin (5x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164856} {:answer/id #uuid "17be339b-6d42-52da-91f6-0ca19e3be727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\sin (4x) - \\frac{1}{10}\\sin (5x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164857} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3eea91-be34-59c3-a8ca-309b6f31b2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\sin (x) + \\frac{1}{6}\\sin (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164858} {:answer/id #uuid "5814c881-6d20-56dc-b07c-808e1798e80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\cos (4x) - \\frac{1}{12}\\cos (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c202af47-5cc0-5423-9bc1-f59200a5ab79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\sin (4x) - \\frac{1}{12}\\sin (6x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164855} :answer-field/id #uuid "8154c11c-70fc-56de-8735-13730e5f9f4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2a49ab5-d314-5040-af8a-5e1f23f419b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340384" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3cb2fcb-3457-5a89-8968-6c0f2c2cfa83" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Cosines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5bd0b00-dd67-5b6b-801d-26023af43170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/mat