n/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14x)-\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d3b7f35-fcd1-5443-b0ea-0b06e016e0c3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b430673b-3b70-58ba-8d7f-7497d1d25af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312750} {:answer/id #uuid "